# `fetch` against the host-side test server (N3; `make test-net-fetch`, which
# turns @PORT@ and the @SUM...@ checksums into numbers). The guest is on QEMU's
# user network without `restrict=on`: 10.0.2.2 is the host's 127.0.0.1, where
# tools/test-httpd listens. The disk's /config/settings says
# `net.hosts = test.zoraya=10.0.2.2`.
! panicked
! first process ended
< Type help to begin, or help tour.
< \r\n~>
# The lease takes a few hundred milliseconds under TCG.
> sleep 3\r
< \r\n~>
# A page, byte for byte, by address.
> fetch http://10.0.2.2:@PORT@/\r
< Zoraya test page

< fetched over HTTP, byte for byte

< \r\n~>
# Bodies of every framing, by checksum: with a length, chunked (by name, which
# `net.hosts` gives), and ended by the close.
> fetch --sum http://10.0.2.2:@PORT@/pattern/1000000\r
< 1000000 bytes fnv1a @SUM1M@ in
< \r\n~>
> fetch --sum http://test.zoraya:@PORT@/chunked/300000\r
< 300000 bytes fnv1a @SUM300K@ in
< \r\n~>
> fetch --sum http://10.0.2.2:@PORT@/close/70000\r
< 70000 bytes fnv1a @SUM70K@ in
< \r\n~>
# More than the rings and the socket buffers hold, from a server that gives it
# slowly and quickly.
> fetch --sum http://10.0.2.2:@PORT@/pattern/4000000\r
< 4000000 bytes fnv1a @SUM4M@ in
< \r\n~>
# A file, written and read back.
> fetch --output page.txt http://10.0.2.2:@PORT@/\r
< \r\n~>
> cat page.txt\r
< Zoraya test page

< fetched over HTTP, byte for byte

< \r\n~>
# `--output` leaves an existing file alone when nothing arrives: a 404, a
# refused connection, a cut page (the file is made at the first byte of a 2xx
# page, not before).
> fetch --output keep.txt http://10.0.2.2:@PORT@/\r
< \r\n~>
> fetch --output keep.txt http://10.0.2.2:@PORT@/status/404\r
< (HTTP 404)
< \r\n~>
> cat keep.txt\r
< Zoraya test page
< fetched over HTTP, byte for byte
< \r\n~>
> fetch --output keep.txt http://10.0.2.2:1/\r
< the connection was refused
< \r\n~>
> cat keep.txt\r
< Zoraya test page
< \r\n~>
> fetch --output cut.bin http://10.0.2.2:@PORT@/reset\r
< the connection ended before the page did
< \r\n~>
> status\r
< \r\nexit status 1
< \r\n~>
> cat cut.bin\r
< cut.bin
< \r\n~>
> status\r
< \r\nexit status 1
< \r\n~>
# A URL from a variable or a substitution does not designate (ADR-0224 item
# 2): the line is refused before it runs.
> set u http://10.0.2.2:@PORT@/\r
< \r\n~>
> fetch $u\r
< from a value, outside the line's network grants
< \r\n~>
> fetch $(echo http://10.0.2.2:@PORT@/)\r
< network grants
< \r\n~>
# N6, ADR-0224 item 1: a `+net` word on the line lets the value designate, and
# only what it covers: the host and port written, not the rest.
> +net 10.0.2.2:@PORT@ fetch $u\r
< Zoraya test page
< \r\n~>
> +net 10.0.2.2:1 fetch $u\r
< from a value, outside the line's network grants
< +net 10.0.2.2:@PORT@ COMMAND
< \r\n~>
> +net nonsense: fetch http://10.0.2.2:@PORT@/\r
< +net nonsense:
< \r\n~>
> grants +net 10.0.2.2:@PORT@ fetch $u\r
< net: 10.0.2.2:@PORT@
< reach (grant +net)
< \r\n~>
> grants fetch http://10.0.2.2:@PORT@/\r
< reach (operand URL)
< \r\n~>
# What goes wrong is said, and the exit status is 1.
> fetch http://10.0.2.2:@PORT@/status/404\r
< fetch: http://10.0.2.2:@PORT@/status/404: the server did not give the page (HTTP 404)
< \r\n~>
> status\r
< \r\nexit status 1
< \r\n~>
> fetch http://10.0.2.2:@PORT@/redirect\r
< the page has moved; redirects are not followed (HTTP 302)
< \r\n~>
> fetch --sum http://10.0.2.2:@PORT@/reset\r
< the connection ended before the page did
< \r\n~>
# Nothing listens on port 1 of the host: SLIRP answers the SYN with a reset.
> fetch http://10.0.2.2:1/\r
< the connection was refused
< \r\n~>
# HTTPS (N5, ADR-0228; the runner's TLS terminators in front of the same
# server, with the test PKI whose root is this disk's /system/tls/roots). The
# date and the randomness are the system's: the certificates are checked
# against `date`'s clock, and the handshake draws on the random service.
> fetch --sum https://test.zoraya:@TLSVALID@/pattern/1000000\r
< 1000000 bytes fnv1a @SUM1M@ in
< \r\n~>
> fetch https://test.zoraya:@TLSVALID@/\r
< Zoraya test page
< fetched over HTTP, byte for byte
< \r\n~>
# Refused before any byte of the request is sent; the Makefile checks the
# server's log for the same.
> fetch https://expired.zoraya:@TLSEXPIRED@/\r
< certificate expired
< \r\n~>
> status\r
< \r\nexit status 1
< \r\n~>
> fetch https://wrong.zoraya:@TLSWRONG@/\r
< certificate is for another name
< \r\n~>
# A plain server on an HTTPS address is no TLS server.
> fetch https://test.zoraya:@PORT@/\r
< the connection ended without a close_notify
< \r\n~>
# The certificate is checked against the name in the address: asked by number,
# the valid server's certificate is for no name that matches.
> fetch https://10.0.2.2:@TLSVALID@/\r
< certificate is for another name
< \r\n~>
# Only http:// and https:// with a host.
> fetch ftp://10.0.2.2/\r
< only http:// and https:// addresses are understood
< \r\n~>
> fetch http://10.0.2.2:99999/\r
< the port is not a number from 1 to 65535
< \r\n~>
# N6, ADR-0221 items 5 and 6 (INV-0221-3, INV-0221-4): this is the test image
# (feature net-test, ADR-0296 item 5) and the volume holds
# /etc/zoraya/net-crash, so `init` started `netd` to end on request. Each
# `net crash` has the shell ask `init`, which sends `Crash` on its control
# connection and so ends the service; `init` restarts the network and the shell gets
# a new root connection on its services channel, so a fetch works again. The
# fourth end is past the three restarts of a boot: the network stays down (the
# refusal has no "(restarting)" after it), and a line that needs it is refused.
# No program is ended by any of it.
> net crash\r
< net: asked the network service to end
< \r\n~>
> sleep 5\r
< \r\n~>
> fetch http://10.0.2.2:@PORT@/\r
< Zoraya test page
< \r\n~>
> net crash\r
< \r\n~>
> sleep 5\r
< \r\n~>
> fetch http://10.0.2.2:@PORT@/\r
< Zoraya test page
< \r\n~>
> net crash\r
< \r\n~>
> sleep 5\r
< \r\n~>
> fetch http://10.0.2.2:@PORT@/\r
< Zoraya test page
< \r\n~>
> net crash\r
< net: asked the network service to end
< \r\n~>
> sleep 1\r
< \r\n~>
> fetch http://10.0.2.2:@PORT@/\r
< zosh: fetch: the network is not available\r\n~>
> net status\r
< zosh: net: the network is not available\r\n~>
x power off
> poweroff\r
